      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sorry about that, I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Think of it more like a trusted, yet slightly mischievous, financial wizard who keeps an eye on the funds and makes sure everything is in order.\n\nNow, to answer your question, when the escrow account is set up, the funds are typically held in a separate account, which is managed by a third-party escrow agent or the title company. The escrow agent holds the funds until the closing date, when the transaction is complete.\n\nOn the closing day, the escrow agent reviews the final documents and ensures that all conditions of the sale have been met. Once everything is in order, the escrow agent releases the funds to the seller, who then uses them to pay off any outstanding debts, such as the mortgage, and to cover any other expenses related to the sale.\n\nIt's a bit like a goblin's treasure chest, where the funds are locked away until the right moment, when the goblin (or in this case, the escrow agent) gives the signal to release the treasure. But, instead of gold coins, it's a large sum of money that's being transferred to the seller.\n\nIn terms of the actual process,",
